On Mon, 12 Oct 2026 11:52:08 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
>> 1. How does a markdown spec mark a :conflict-plan: heading? The skill
>>    assumes a heading whose text ends with ":conflict-plan:".
>
> This should now be addressed in the spec.

It is. A plan is now a heading prefixed with "conflict plan:", which
works the same in org and markdown. The skill on b/dev, in
"claude/skills/spec-report: New skill", already looks for
"* conflict plan: ..." in org and "## conflict plan: ..." in markdown.

>> 2. spec.org:19 lists REPORT--*.org as an example, but the rule at
>>    spec.org:20 turns SPEC--*.md into REPORT--*.md. The skill follows
>>    the rule.
>
> I don't understand what the issue is. I've updated the spec to hopefully
> be more clear. Review those changes, and if there are still issues,
> provide clear examples of the confusing scenario so I can update the
> spec to prevent it.

I reviewed the updated spec. The example worked through in "Re:
[PATCH v3 2/4] claude/skills/spec-report: Initial spec" [1] still
applies, now at spec.org:22-25:

    - Produce a sibling report file (e.g. =report.org=, =REPORT.md=,
      =report--<name>.org=, =REPORT--<name>.org=) corresponding to the
      spec file
      - report file name is produced by =s/^spec/report/ <spec>=

There are two issues.

First, the extension. The spec forms at spec.org:11-12 are spec.org,
SPEC.md, spec--<name>.org, and SPEC--<name>.md. None of them produces
REPORT--<name>.org. A reader matching the examples one to one expects
SPEC--cli.md to give REPORT--cli.org, but the rule gives
REPORT--cli.md. Changing the fourth example to =REPORT--<name>.md=
makes the two agree.

Second, the case. Read literally, s/^spec/report/ is case-sensitive,
so it does nothing to the upper-case forms:

    $ for f in spec.org SPEC.md spec--cli.org SPEC--cli.md; do
    >     printf '%s -> %s\n' "$f" "$(echo "$f" | sed 's/^spec/report/')"
    > done
    spec.org -> report.org
    SPEC.md -> SPEC.md
    spec--cli.org -> report--cli.org
    SPEC--cli.md -> SPEC--cli.md

An implementation that follows the rule to the letter names the
report of SPEC.md "SPEC.md", and the report overwrites the spec. The
examples REPORT.md and REPORT--<name>.* show that you meant to keep
the case, and the skill does that with a second substitution,
s/^SPEC/REPORT/. A rule that names both cases would close the gap,
e.g. "replace the leading spec with report, keeping its case (SPEC
becomes REPORT)", or =s/^spec/report/; s/^SPEC/REPORT/=.

Neither issue changes what the skill does today. Both are in your
commit on b/dev, so I have left the wording to you rather than post a
patch. I'll send one if you would like.

Thanks.

